     It is explicitly coded so that you can only change alignment if
your base alignment (which might differ from your current alignment
depending upon equipment you're using) matches your original alignment.
Once you've converted, that condition can never be met again so it is
impossible to convert a second time, either back to your original or
to the third choice.  And it's been like that ever since alignment,
altars, and #offer were put into the game.  However, back when it was
first done there was no "you can't get there from here" aspect to it.
So whether it's a conscious design now is not quite so cut and dried.
On the other hand, devteam is aware of this issue and hasn't attempted
to change it so evidently they don't feel as strongly as the people
who occasionally wring their hands over it.  No one expects to win
every game; the few who seem to be gradually approaching that point
have learned to avoid this trivially avoidable problem.

     As to whether it's evident that changing alignment is generally
a bad thing to do, the initial screen says right out that you've been
chosen as emissary for <deity> to obtain the Amulet.  It shouldn't be
a big surprise that switching to one of the rival deities poses a risk
of bad consequences.


